WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.810 --> 00:00:05.450
Orphan elephants need a lot of affection.

00:00:05.450 --> 00:00:10.660
Every day, the carers at the IFAW Wildlife
Rescue Center spend several hours playing

00:00:10.660 --> 00:00:12.840
and cuddling with them.

00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:18.560
Experience has shown that otherwise, they
waste away.

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:31.710
The center is also home to 13 baby rhinos,
some Indian hog deer, leopards and various

00:00:31.710 --> 00:00:34.170
types of ape.

00:00:34.170 --> 00:00:38.320
Most of them come from the nearby Kaziranga
National Park.

00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:43.500
This baby elephant was rescued from a ditch
it had got stuck in.

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:46.190
That sort of thing happens often.

00:00:46.190 --> 00:00:53.330
Traffic accidents are also common.

00:00:53.330 --> 00:00:56.560
Baby elephants don't have very robust immune
systems.

00:00:56.560 --> 00:00:59.120
We need to be very careful with them.

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:03.310
We used to make sure there was always a carer
with them, but it's too risky.

00:01:03.310 --> 00:01:07.750
They get their trunks into everything and
they can easily pick up infections from humans.

00:01:07.750 --> 00:01:13.511
Dr. Basumatary is keen to limit
contact between the baby elephants and their

00:01:13.511 --> 00:01:27.000
carers - without depriving the calves of the
bodily warmth they need to survive.

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:31.210
These two baby elephants are now sharing their
quarters.

00:01:31.210 --> 00:01:35.069
There's a yard they can play in, and a roofed
hut where they can sleep.

00:01:35.069 --> 00:01:52.109
At night, when temperatures drop, they even
wear pajamas and slippers.

00:01:52.109 --> 00:01:56.750
We do our best to look after the calves in
a species-appropriate way.

00:01:56.750 --> 00:02:00.380
They're kept outside to recreate forest life
with their mothers.

00:02:00.380 --> 00:02:05.209
It also helps them adjust when they're released.

00:02:05.209 --> 00:02:08.149
In Assam, animals and humans live in close
proximity.

00:02:08.149 --> 00:02:13.819
The road connecting the Kaziranga National
Park with the veterinary clinic is one of

00:02:13.819 --> 00:02:15.709
the busiest in the region.

00:02:15.709 --> 00:02:23.340
The state's main industries are tourism and
agriculture.

00:02:23.340 --> 00:02:29.030
Working elephants are a common sight.

00:02:29.030 --> 00:02:32.110
Tea plantations line the hill slopes.

00:02:32.110 --> 00:02:38.739
Assam tea is world famous.

00:02:38.739 --> 00:02:41.939
On the other side of the road are rice plantations.

00:02:41.939 --> 00:02:49.720
Assam is flood-prone, so harvests are often
ruined.

00:02:49.720 --> 00:02:54.620
The floods come twice a year.

00:02:54.620 --> 00:02:57.420
The Gogoi family are making rice flour.

00:02:57.420 --> 00:03:01.950
A few repairs are needed on the house.

00:03:01.950 --> 00:03:10.989
In autumn, a herd of deer escaping the rising
flood water sought refuge on their land.

00:03:10.989 --> 00:03:14.829
Down there is our village, Mohpora.

00:03:14.829 --> 00:03:19.860
Less than a kilometre in that direction is
the national park.

00:03:19.860 --> 00:03:21.610
It's home to wild animals.

00:03:21.610 --> 00:03:25.950
Rhinos, elephants, tigers, water buffalo and
many others.

00:03:25.950 --> 00:03:31.840
If the river bursts its banks it puts their
lives in danger.

00:03:31.840 --> 00:03:36.689
And then they come to the village.

00:03:36.689 --> 00:03:39.930
Two thirds of his last harvest was ruined.

00:03:39.930 --> 00:03:44.819
He had to resort to planting mustard seeds,
which grow fast, to make sure he had at least

00:03:44.819 --> 00:03:48.830
some income.

00:03:48.830 --> 00:03:50.200
Our problems are mounting.

00:03:50.200 --> 00:03:55.629
The tea plantations are expanding across the
slopes, reducing the forest - the elephants'

00:03:55.629 --> 00:03:56.629
habitat.

00:03:56.629 --> 00:03:58.489
So they come down here and ruin our crops.

00:03:58.489 --> 00:04:01.530
They destroy our homes and kill people.

00:04:01.530 --> 00:04:04.890
The water buffalo also leave the park in search
of food.

00:04:04.890 --> 00:04:08.150
The tigers prey on their calves, and go crazy.

00:04:08.150 --> 00:04:11.939
And kill more people.

00:04:11.939 --> 00:04:13.969
Tiger attacks ocurr every month.

00:04:13.969 --> 00:04:19.280
Phulmai Choudhury's father-in-law suffered
one on his way to work one morning.

00:04:19.280 --> 00:04:23.130
Her husband can't bear to talk about it.

00:04:23.130 --> 00:04:29.330
My father in law was badly wounded.

00:04:29.330 --> 00:04:31.660
A woman from the village found him.

00:04:31.660 --> 00:04:34.190
He was carried back home on a blanket.

00:04:34.190 --> 00:04:42.660
We took him to hospital but he'd already lost
so much blood, and died the same day.

00:04:42.660 --> 00:04:46.430
Children aren't allowed to play outside in
the mornings and evenings, when the danger's

00:04:46.430 --> 00:04:48.420
most acute.

00:04:48.420 --> 00:04:54.180
In the National park the animals are allowed
to roam free.

00:04:54.180 --> 00:04:57.700
Its rhinoceros population is one of the most
stable in the world.

00:04:57.700 --> 00:05:02.220
But to locals, it feels like the government
cares more about wildlife than it does about

00:05:02.220 --> 00:05:10.970
humans.

00:05:10.970 --> 00:05:14.340
That's why they're taking matters into their
own hands.

00:05:14.340 --> 00:05:18.569
Every night, local men patrol the border to
the national park.

00:05:18.569 --> 00:05:23.330
They use torches to scare off any roving animals.

00:05:23.330 --> 00:05:27.220
Torches wouldn't be much use if the animals
turned aggressive.

00:05:27.220 --> 00:05:32.460
But their families in the village feel safer.

00:05:32.460 --> 00:05:40.990
The 
elephants have to get used to living in the

00:05:40.990 --> 00:05:42.330
wild.

00:05:42.330 --> 00:05:46.910
The baby elephants born in the national park
have grown into a herd.

00:05:46.910 --> 00:05:51.550
They're taken out every day to learn how to
look for food - and also to avoid villages

00:05:51.550 --> 00:05:53.110
and roads.

00:05:53.110 --> 00:06:00.889
Next year, they'll be moved to another national
park where they'll roam free.

00:06:00.889 --> 00:06:10.849
We raise them, feeding them with bottles.

00:06:10.849 --> 00:06:17.169
It's always very sad when we have to take
them to the Madras Park.

00:06:17.169 --> 00:06:22.349
Sometimes I go there and call them - and they
come and see me.

00:06:22.349 --> 00:06:24.530
It's good to see them in their habitat.

00:06:24.530 --> 00:06:32.940
In the wild, where they belong.

00:06:32.940 --> 00:06:35.260
But that's still a long way off.

00:06:35.260 --> 00:06:38.550
For the time being, the herd still has a lot
to learn.

